.TH AWK 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
awk \- pattern scanning and processing language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B awk
.RI [ \-F " sepstring" ]
.RI [ \-v " assignment" ]...
.I program
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B awk
.RI [ \-F " sepstring" ]
.RI [ \-v " assignment" ]...
.B \-f
.I progfile
.RB [ \-f
.IR progfile ]...
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B awk
runs a program written in the
.B awk
language against each
.I argument
that names a file (or standard input, if none does), reading one
record at a time and executing every pattern-action pair in the
program whose pattern matches that record.
.PP
An
.B awk
program is a sequence of pattern/action pairs, written
.RI pattern " { " action " }" ,
and function definitions. Either the pattern or the action may be
omitted: a pattern with no action prints the matching record as its
default action, and an action with no pattern (or the special pattern
.BR BEGIN " or " END )
runs unconditionally \(em
.B BEGIN
once before the first record is read,
.B END
once after the last one. A pattern is a boolean expression, the
special word
.BR BEGIN " or " END ,
or a range pattern, written
.RI expr1 , expr2 ,
matching every record from one where
.I expr1
first becomes true through the next one where
.I expr2
becomes true, inclusive.
.SS "Records and fields"
Each input record is split into fields on the separator given by the
variable
.BR FS " (default a single space, meaning \(lqsplit on runs of blanks,"
and ignore leading/trailing blanks\(rq, per the standard). A field is
referenced as
.BR $1 ", " $2 ,
and so on;
.B $0
is the whole record, and
.B NF
is the number of fields. Assigning to
.B $0
re-splits it into fields; assigning to a field beyond
.B NF
extends the record, joining fields with
.BR OFS ;
assigning to
.B NF
itself truncates or extends the field list the same way.
.B NR
counts records read so far across all input;
.B FNR
counts records read from the current input file; records are
terminated by the character
.BR RS " (only its first character is consulted, even if it is set to"
a longer string). Setting
.B RS
to the empty string selects paragraph mode: records are separated by
one or more blank lines, and, in addition to
.BR FS ,
a newline always also separates fields when
.B FS
is a single character (including the default) \(em though not when
.B FS
is a longer, regular-expression separator.
.SS "Statements"
The full statement set is implemented:
.RS
.nf
if (\fIcond\fP) \fIstmt\fP [else \fIstmt\fP]
while (\fIcond\fP) \fIstmt\fP
do \fIstmt\fP while (\fIcond\fP)
for (\fIinit\fP; \fIcond\fP; \fIincr\fP) \fIstmt\fP
for (\fIvar\fP in \fIarray\fP) \fIstmt\fP
break
continue
next
exit [\fIexpr\fP]
return [\fIexpr\fP]
delete \fIarray\fP[\fIsubscript\fP]
delete \fIarray\fP                  (removes every element)
print [\fIexpr\fP, ...] [redirection]
printf \fIfmt\fP [, \fIexpr\fP, ...] [redirection]
function \fIname\fP(\fIparams\fP) { \fIstmts\fP }
.fi
.RE
where
.I redirection
is one of
\(lq> \fIfile\fR\(rq, \(lq>> \fIfile\fR\(rq, or \(lq| \fIcmd\fR\(rq
on
.BR print " or " printf .
A function is called by name elsewhere in the program (recursively,
if desired); within a called function, a bare-identifier argument is
passed by reference if it is (or, being still uninitialized, becomes)
an array, and by value if it is a scalar.
.SS "Expressions"
The full operator set is implemented: arithmetic
.RB ( "+ \- * / % ^" ),
string concatenation (by adjacency), the relational operators
.RB ( "< <= == != >= >" ),
.BR ~ " and " !~
(match/non-match against a regular expression, given either as a
literal
.I /ere/
or as a computed string), the logical operators
.BR && ", " || ", and " ! ,
the ternary
.RB \(lq ?: \(rq ,
assignment
.RB ( "= += \-= *= /= %= ^=" ),
pre/post increment and decrement
.RB ( "++ \-\-" ),
array membership
.RI ( subscript " in " array ),
and every form of
.BR getline :
.RS
.nf
getline                (updates $0, NF, NR, FNR)
getline \fIvar\fP             (updates \fIvar\fP, NR, FNR)
getline < \fIfile\fP          (updates $0, NF)
getline \fIvar\fP < \fIfile\fP     (updates \fIvar\fP only)
\fIcmd\fP | getline           (updates $0, NF, NR)
\fIcmd\fP | getline \fIvar\fP        (updates \fIvar\fP, NR)
.fi
.RE
.SS "Built-in functions"
.RS
.nf
length([\fIs\fP])              int(\fIx\fP)
substr(\fIs\fP, \fIm\fP [, \fIn\fP])       rand()
index(\fIs\fP, \fIt\fP)             srand([\fIseed\fP])
split(\fIs\fP, \fIarray\fP [, \fIfs\fP])   tolower(\fIs\fP)
sub(\fIere\fP, \fIrepl\fP [, \fItarget\fP])  toupper(\fIs\fP)
gsub(\fIere\fP, \fIrepl\fP [, \fItarget\fP]) close(\fIname\fP)
match(\fIs\fP, \fIere\fP)            system(\fIcmd\fP)
sprintf(\fIfmt\fP [, \fIexpr\fP, ...])
sin(\fIx\fP)  cos(\fIx\fP)  atan2(\fIy\fP, \fIx\fP)  exp(\fIx\fP)  log(\fIx\fP)  sqrt(\fIx\fP)
.fi
.RE
Every one of these is a mandatory POSIX
.B awk
built-in; none is a gawk or BWK extension.
.SS "printf/sprintf conversions"
.B print
writes its arguments separated by
.B OFS
and terminated by
.BR ORS .
.BR printf " and " sprintf
support the conversions
.BR d ,
.I i
(a synonym for
.BR d ),
.BR o ,
.BR u ,
.BR x ,
.BR X ,
.BR e ,
.BR E ,
.BR f ,
.BR F ,
.BR g ,
.BR G ,
.BR c ,
and
.BR s .
Each takes the usual
\(lq\-+0#\(rq
flags (and a literal blank flag), a field width, and a precision,
either given literally or as
.RB \(lq * \(rq
to take the next argument's value; a literal
.RB \(lq %% \(rq
in the format string produces one
.RB \(lq % \(rq
in the output. An unrecognized conversion letter is copied through to
the output literally rather than silently dropped. No C length
modifier
.RB ( "h hh l ll L" )
is meaningful or accepted, since every
.B awk
value is already either a number or a string, never a distinctly
sized C type.
.SS "Special variables"
.RS
.nf
FS OFS ORS RS NR NF FNR FILENAME SUBSEP RSTART RLENGTH
CONVFMT OFMT ARGC ARGV ENVIRON
.fi
.RE
.B ENVIRON
is populated from this process's own environment before
.B BEGIN
runs.
.BR ARGV / ARGC
hold the operands following the program text (index 0 of
.B ARGV
is the program's invocation name), and elements of
.B ARGV
may be reassigned or added to during execution to change which files
are read.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-F " sepstring"
Sets
.B FS
to
.I sepstring
before
.B BEGIN
runs, exactly as if
\(lqFS=\fIsepstring\fR\(rq
had been given as a
.B \-v
assignment.
.TP
.BI \-v " assignment"
An assignment of the form
.RI \(lq name = value \(rq ,
applied to variable
.I name
before
.B BEGIN
runs. May be repeated; assignments are applied in the order given,
so a later one overrides an earlier one naming the same variable.
Given together with
.B \-F
naming the same variable, whichever option appears later on the
command line wins (the standard does not specify an order between
the two; this implementation simply processes every option
left to right).
.TP
.BI \-f " progfile"
Read the program text from
.I progfile
instead of from the first operand. May be repeated; each file's text
is concatenated, in the order given, to form the complete program (a
newline is inserted between files if one is missing). Once any
.B \-f
is given, there is no
.I program
operand: every operand is instead an
.IR argument .
.RB \(lq \- \(rq
as a
.I progfile
reads the program text from standard input.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I program
The program text, taken from the first operand when no
.B \-f
option was given.
.TP
.I argument
Either a
.I file
to read records from, or, if it has the form
.RI \(lq name = value \(rq
where
.I name
is a valid identifier, an assignment to variable
.I name
that takes effect at the point the main input loop reaches that
.I argument
in
.B ARGV
order (not all at once before processing begins) \(em so a variable
assigned this way between two file operands has its old value while
the first file is read and its new value while the second is. With no
.I file
arguments at all, standard input is read.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
Numeric literals in the program text are decimal only, matching the
standard's own grammar; there is no hexadecimal or octal literal
syntax, so
.RB \(lq 0x1 \(rq
in program source lexes as the number
.B 0
concatenated with the identifier
.BR x1 ,
not as the number 1.
.PP
An empty
.B FS
(\(lqsplit every character into its own field\(rq, a common
extension) is not implemented; with
.B FS
set to the empty string, the whole record is treated as a single
field instead.
.B nextfile
and
.B fflush()
are gawk/BWK extensions with no citation in the base standard and are
not implemented; every stream this implementation itself opens is
still flushed automatically at the moments that matter (before
.B system()
or
\(lq| getline\(rq,
and at normal program exit).
.PP
The iteration order of
.RI "for (" var " in " array )
is, as the standard itself leaves it, unspecified; it follows this
implementation's own hash table's internal layout.
.PP
Any fatal condition \(em a division by zero, an array used as a
scalar or vice versa, a call to an undefined function, an invalid
computed regular expression, a failed output redirection, or memory
exhaustion \(em is reported with a diagnostic and ends the program
with a nonzero exit status rather than continuing in an undefined
state.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The program ran to completion (including a normal
.B exit
statement with no argument, or with an argument of 0).
.TP
.I n
An
.B exit
statement with a nonzero argument
.I n
sets that value as the exit status.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error occurred (an invalid option, a missing program or
.B \-f
file), the program text failed to parse, or a fatal runtime error
occurred (see APPLICATION USAGE).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sed (1),
.BR grep (1),
.BR m4 (1)
